[{:seon.task/name "config-is-schema-defaults-and-datoms"
  :seon.task/plan "PLAN, rewritten 2026-11-16 by runtime after reading every path (owner: \"find all duplication and the simple solution\"). THE CAUSE: two different things share one map. SETTINGS (bounds, model, provider, limits: the same for every process on a branch) and PROCESS FACTS (ports, store path, runs dir, main's name, source root, checkout paths, cores, end-with, embed model path, the profile: different per process). Every arm of seon.config exists because process facts were put in the branch's config: places (24 keys a branch must not move), at-load and waiting, #env and #seon/checkout, config/local.edn, --config and --set, aero, built-text, hold-args!, the alter-var-roots. DUPLICATION FOUND [read]: the config held in SIX copies: built-text/loaded (seon.config, baked default.edn read by aero); the held atom; the config and sources vars (alter-var-root); the branch's :config/key/:config/form text entities (seon.source config-tx import, read by seon.config/entries); main's config entity (seon.task/config-tx, written by seon.writer on a config move, read by seon.config/from-db, whose only src readers are provider-at and the config panel); test-system-config (the file again under :scripted). The text read by aero FIVE times: built-layers at load, of-entries at every hold, hold-args!, config-refused before a write, test-system-config. FOUR override routes: #env, local.edn, --config, --set. TWO validations: of-entries and config-refused, beside the one write path's schema check. THE SIMPLE SOLUTION. (1) A SETTING is one entity per key, {:config/key k :config/value v}, v its EDN value (encoded once at its write, decoded once when the config is derived), on the branch: the existing :config entity with :config/form (text read by aero) replaced by :config/value; :config/file stays, the settings file the key lives in (resources/seon/config/default.edn, plain EDN once (A) and (B) take its tags out), so a merged setting reaches git by the write-back as every file does. The one merge already merges it per key (seon.merge stated-keys' :config kind: :stated becomes {:config/value :value :config/file :file}, :needs :file kept; agreed with data 2026-11-16: a both-changed key is one conflict at [:datum e :config/value], encoding once makes a same value exact; seon.source/changed-files and seon.merge/divergence keep reading :file; seon.merge-test/a-config-entry-merges-per-key rewritten to :config/value and seen red). A key no entity sets is its schema :default. A change is a transaction through the one write path; a new key is a schema entry, no rebuild. Main's separate config entity (seon.task/config-tx, seon.config/from-db, :seon.config/sources, seon.writer's re-write on a move) is the duplicate and goes. (2) A process's settings value = (seon.config/of-branch db role): schema defaults, then the branch's :config/value entities, then its role's profile, recomputed only when a commit touches a config attribute; (cfg/value k) is a map get. (3) PROCESS FACTS are flow's start map's :process (agreed with flow, task seon.step/roots-procs-are-the-first-forks-graph (vii)): {:ports {:server :web} :store :runs :main :source-root :checkout :cores :end-with :embed-model}, given to each proc as Flow :args, never stored on a branch, never in the settings map. Until flow's rebuild lands, the seam (seon.config/process) reads the same map once from argv and environment at the system process's start; every fork inherits it. Paths fixed under the checkout or the runs dir (system-path, tasks-dir, public-dir, stylesheet, probe-lib, vector/lib, generations-dir, agent/cwd, source/branch) are no keys: derived where read from :checkout or :runs. (4) A PROFILE (today only :scripted: provider, script, sole-writer?, private-checked?, vector/texts, wiki-baseline) is {:config/profile p :config/key k :config/value v} entities; the process's role picks it (the agent value's :role, which each proc keeps from its :args). DELETES: default.edn (its three build keys, jolt-compiler-options, jolt-cache-mb, tree-excludes, which bin/env and bin/tree read as text, move to one build-input file until those scripts go), aero for config, built-text, layers, argv/parse-set/--config/--set, local.edn as a config layer (its ports become start args), :config/form and its aero read (the import writes :config/value), the held atom, the config and sources vars, hold!/hold-args!/of-entries/entries-text/text-refusal/config-refused, the place/at-load/waiting arms and cfg/waiting, test-system-config (the test profile's value), :seon.config/sources (a key is set or default), the gate-checkout derivation. Nothing removed in function: every key still has its value, every process still gets its ports and paths, a test still runs scripted. COST (arithmetic): a read one map get ~200 ticks; a commit touching config: an AEVT read of :config/value (~40 entities set beyond defaults) and their decode ~0.2M ticks; a commit touching none 0; a test child 0 (forked with its agent's value; its role's profile merged once per db, ~4 keys); a start: argv read, microseconds. MEASURED [M]: a hold on a branch move ~6.7M ticks; a test child's hold 8.1M ticks / 23 ms (result/d10c5747); a layered read 0.66M; 21 direct var readers in src, 288 calls of value/resolve. ORDER, each landing deleting what it replaces: (A) process facts out: start-facts and its readers; the 24 place keys and every #env/#seon/checkout/#seon/cores/#seon/own-branch/#ref/#join key leave the settings map; local.edn's ports become start args; the place, at-load and waiting arms go. Then default.edn holds only #profile values and the build keys. (B) settings as :config/value entities, profiles as entities: of-branch the one read, from-db and config-tx deleted, the held atom, vars, aero, default.edn, :config/form, test-system-config go. PATHS each runs on: the system process's boot and commit notices, the writer process, agents, test children, the candidate binary's boot, the merge. PROOF: a setting transacted on a branch is read by that branch's next eval and by no other branch; a test child reads the test profile with no parse; a commit touching no config attribute recomputes nothing. (A) IN DETAIL [read, main 13acaf6964]: the schema already classifies a process fact: :seon.config/place, 23 keys. (A) makes a place key read only from start-facts, never from branch entries (of-entries' place arm and its `built` re-decode go). Not ported, since their readers are being deleted: source/gate-checkout and flow's source/main and source/root reads (jolt/flow 87bd7d9580, the run-on-checkout chain); proofs/check-store and proofs/seeds have no src reader (bin/check, bin/test, tests2's deletions); proofs/results-dir, program-store, jobs, tests-dir go with tests2's file cache and bin/test. Settings among today's places (ai/provider, ai/script, store/sole-writer?, vector/texts) are profile values, step (B). STATUS (runtime, 2026-11-16, paused by root): on main: (A) seon.config/process; B1 process-of (computed once at start, kept in held: 303 ticks a read), the 7 place keys out of default.edn; B2 the 6 derived paths deleted and seon.config/storable (a key the store has not declared is left out of the boot fact and named). NEXT when woken: tasks-dir and source/branch into :process (agree the keys with flow), then B3 (script overrides as plain defaults and --set), B4 (profiles as entries, role from flow's start map), B5 (aero, built-layers, held, hold-args!/held-for/install!/test-held!, the place/at-load/waiting arms deleted; :config/form -> :config/value with seon.merge's :config kind and merge-test, data agreed; the :seon.config/config map derived from the seon.config.* schema entries, the two-places class)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-keys-levels-are-its-schemas"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner ruling 2026-10-29 (docs/decisions.md, config at every level, no env var switches): a setting can be set at every level, in this order, later wins: shipped defaults < profile (--profile) < --config FILE < --set < main's root entity < model < provider < task < agent; and each key's schema names the levels it may be set at. What exists: `:seon.task/config` is a closed map of six config keys, a second list beside the schemas; ``:seon.config/place`` marks per-process keys; nothing says which keys may be set at runtime, per model, per task or per agent. Change: schema entries carry ``:seon.config/levels``, a new key, a subset of #{:boot :runtime :model :provider :task :agent} (default #{:boot}: shipped, profile, --config, --set); `:seon.task/config`'s and the agent's keys are derived from the keys whose levels have :task / :agent (the six get :task); `seon.config/resolve-in` and task declaration refuse a key set at a level its schema does not name, with an error naming key, level and the levels it has; the config panel shows each key's levels; docs/config.md gets a table of keys by level generated by the same read. Replaces the hand list in seon.task.edn. Proof: a task declaring a :boot-only key is refused; a :task key resolves with source [:task]; every key in the six-key list has :task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/a-key-set-at-a-level-its-schema-excludes-is-refused seon.config-test/every-task-config-key-has-the-task-level}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-resolver-reads-main-s-copy"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `cfg/value` (121 call sites) reads the boot map; only cfg/provider-at merges `seon.config/from-db` main, so a change to main's root entity applies to the provider alone, though the ruling puts main's root entity above --set and below the model. Change: `seon.config/resolve-in` takes the config in force as `(merge config (from-db main))` keyed by main's commit (a reference, not a held db: docs/storage.md#a-kept-db-value-is-a-reference), so the runtime layer applies to every key whose levels have :runtime, with source [:main]; `seon.config/sources` names it; the three `(get cfg/config k)` bypasses (root.clj; fork_server.clj; my/root.clj) go through `cfg/value`. Measure: calls per turn and bytes of a resolve before/after (a cost proof, work not time). Deletes provider-at's own merge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/a-keys-levels-are-its-schemas"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/a-runtime-change-on-main-applies-to-the-next-read seon.config-test/provider-at-is-the-resolver}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-can-carry-its-own-config"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner ruling 2026-10-29: the agent is a level beside the task, above it (config 'added to an agent's entity to override on a per agent level'). What exists: an agent is a forked process running one task; its identity for the resolver is the task id, and no level is its own. Change: ctx gains `:agent`; `seon.config/resolve-in` gets one clause above the task's, reading the declared :seon.config.* attributes of the agent's run entity whose key names :agent in its levels (the same flattening task.clj does for a task); `(my.root/run! sys [id] {:config {...}})` writes them; `seon.config/sources` reports [:agent]. Reuses the one read; adds one clause and no attribute type. Proof: two runs of one task with different :seon.config.ai/model each use theirs, and a key without :agent in its levels is refused there."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/the-resolver-reads-main-s-copy"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/an-agent-s-setting-wins-over-its-task-s seon.system-test/two-runs-of-a-task-use-their-own-model}}
 {:seon.task/name "per-run-keys-ride-the-command-line"
  :seon.task/plan "Curated 2026-11-15 by context (root's ruling: a task's work is never a bin/ shell script; bin/ is a thin wrapper over an op, AGENTS §0): the work is each per-run key as an argument of the op the thin wrapper calls (bin/mcp call OP KEY=VALUE), not bin/ scripts' environment; it waits on THE PLAN item 13. Owner ruling 2026-10-29: bin/ passes --set, not env vars. What exists: bin/test, bin/check, bin/system, bin/env and test_store.clj set SEON_WIKI_BASELINE, RUNS, STORE, MAIN, SOURCE_ROOT, RESULTS_DIR, COST_MAX_N, SEEDS, EMBED_TEXTS, WEB_PORT, SERVER_PORT, INSTRUMENT, AI_MODEL, RUN_CHECKOUT, which default.edn reads with `#env` under --set (the stale-local.edn failure docs/config.md already names); fx.clj strips the leaked ones from test children; config/local.edn holds two `#env`. Change: delete the remaining `#env` tags and local.edn's two (bin/ports writes plain port values); bin/ scripts and test_store pass `--set`, as they already pass `--profile`; one function, to be written (``seon.config/args``), builds a child's `--set` list from the keys marked ``:seon.config/place`` (read in seon.fx, not a registered schema key); the strip lists keep only launcher variables; the no-env-tag test's exception set (SEON_AI_MODEL, SEON_INSTRUMENT) empties and the test becomes `default.edn reads no environment`. Measure: verdict reach digests move once (state it), `bin/check config` green. Deletes: 14 env exports, the strip entries, config.md's warning."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/default-edn-reads-no-environment seon.config-test/a-child-gets-its-keys-as-set-arguments seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-plain-ports}}
 {:seon.task/name "config-page-lists-levels-env-and-process-knobs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: docs/config.md has layers, the profile and the resolver but no table of levels, of the environment variables that stay (SEON_CHECKOUT, the API-key variable, launcher JOLT_*/HOME), or of process knobs (machine.md has the heap ones). Change, finished here after the tasks above: one 'Levels' table (the ruling's order, each level's source name), one 'Environment' table (each remaining variable, who reads it, why), one 'Process settings' table (JOLT_MAX_HEAP/NEW_SIZE, MAX_HEAP_FREE_RATIO, sweepers, COMPILER_OPTIONS, native libs: key, edge function, process, when), linked from machine.md, model.md (SEON_AI_MODEL becomes --set) and the default.edn header. bin/system drops its repeated JOLT_*_LIB line by sourcing bin/env with the tree's ROOT. Proof: a test reads each table entry's key and edge function and checks it exists."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/per-run-keys-ride-the-command-line" "seon.config/an-agent-can-carry-its-own-config"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/every-process-knob-names-its-key-and-edge-function}}
 {:seon.task/name "config-is-branch-data-read-through-the-env"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan Stage 2.1 (config as branch data read through the env). What exists: seon.config/config and seon.config/sources are defs taken once at load (config.clj), and 61 readers in src and test take them directly instead of through seon.config/value (measured 2026-11-14, grep of cfg/config and config/config). A process forked from another (a test child of the service, a fork of root's agent) therefore read its parent's config: --profile deepseek, local.edn's source root and branch (runtime's prove read main's jolt/merges; the armed-call unit measured 6.1 ticks against 15.1 under scripted). The bridge, seon.config/hold-checkout!, alter-var-roots both defs in the test child to the checkout's test config. Cause: a value resolved at load and kept in a var is state the branch doesn't name. Change: a process's config is its environment's ({:branch :commit :t}): the config entries on its branch, read through the one resolver (seon.config/value) under that env; every direct reader of the load-time defs moves to it; the defs remain only as what the binary loaded with (the value a branch's differs from). Deletes: seon.config/hold-checkout! and its docs/resolution.md entry, and seon.fx/checkout-env! once no run is of another checkout (DELETIONS.md row). Proof: a law that no reader outside seon.config takes seon.config/config, and a forked process on another branch reads that branch's config."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/no-reader-outside-config-takes-the-load-time-config seon.config-test/a-forked-process-reads-its-branchs-config}} {:seon.task/name "a-config-or-env-read-records-its-entry"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by deps for runtime, root's call, 2026-11-14. Root's one-tracker direction, agreed 2026-11-14 (target/root/one-derivation-survey.html): every read a computation makes records through Datahike's di/record-read! as a datom key, so a reaction or a reused verdict is never green on a read nobody recorded (a false green). What exists [M, counts in src]: 253 calls of seon.config/value, all through the one resolver, read a resolved map, so no datom read is recorded; 2 System/getenv calls read the environment directly. Change: the resolver records [:e the :config entry's entity] for each key it answers while a run records; an environment variable a run reads is a datom first (the import or start-process! writes it), read through the resolver. Small: one recording site plus the two getenv sites. Proof, red first: a reaction that reads a config key reruns when that key's entry changes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/a-config-or-env-read-records-its-entry}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
]
